    {
      "key": "distribution",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_macronotacanthus.htm",
      "html": "<p>South and southeast Asia: Bay of Bengal and western central Pacific; in coastal waters and estuaries. Type locality: Batavia.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "habitat",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_macronotacanthus.htm",
      "html": "<p>It inhabits southern and southeastern Asian brackish waters, also occurring in marine and freshwaters but predominantly in brackish waters.</p>"
    },
    {
      "key": "notes",
      "source": "scotcat",
      "sourceUrl": "https://www.scotcat.com/ariidae/arius_macronotacanthus.htm",
      "html": "<p>Described by Pieter Bleeker in 1846, originally under the genus Arius; after two genus and specific name changes it has returned to its original description. It can sometimes be found in online searches as Nemapteryx macronotacantha.</p><p>Etymology: the specific name macronotacanthus comes from macro-, large; notos, back; acanthus, spine, referring to the thick dorsal spine.</p>"
    }
